  and a documented dev fallback (console email, mock push), never a crash.

## 1. Alert pipeline (server)
Migrations:
- alert_deliveries (id, alert_id, channel: in_app|email|telegram|push, status:
  queued|sent|failed|skipped, attempts, next_attempt_at, last_error_code (no
  PII), sent_at)
- telegram_links (guardian_id, chat_id, link_token_hash, linked_at)
- push_subscriptions (user_id, endpoint, keys, created_at, last_seen_at)
- alert_escalations (alert_id, escalated_at, to_guardian_id)
- cloud_usage (user_id, day, gemini_calls, safe_browsing_calls)
- explanation_cache (key = hash of sorted signalIds + verdict + language,
  text, created_at)
- Add columns where needed: user_preferences.cloud_explanations_enabled
  (default false), user_preferences.cloud_url_check_enabled (default false),
  notification_preferences additions (include_elder_name bool, quiet_hours,
  dangerous_bypasses_quiet_hours default true, escalation_minutes default 15).
RLS on everything; follow the Layer 3 helper-function pattern.

Flow:
1. When sync-incident stores an incident with verdict DANGEROUS (and
   BE_CAREFUL only if the guardian opted in), it creates guardian_alerts for
   every ACTIVE connection whose share_level allows it, plus queued
   alert_deliveries per channel the guardian enabled.
2. Dedupe: if an alert with the same elder and the same sorted signal set
   exists within the last 30 minutes, attach to it instead of creating a new
   one (avoid alert fatigue).
3. A scheduled worker function (pg_cron or Supabase scheduled function)
   processes queued deliveries with exponential backoff (max 5 attempts) and
   records status per channel. Document how to schedule it.
4. Escalation: if an alert is not "seen" within the guardian's
   escalation_minutes, resend through the next enabled channel, and if the
   elder has another active guardian, notify them too. Record in
   alert_escalations.
5. Channels:
   - in_app: realtime via Supabase Realtime plus a notifications list.
   - email: provider abstraction (EmailProvider interface). Implement one
     real provider adapter (for example Resend) and a console adapter for dev.
     Plain-language templates in English and Malayalam based on the
     guardian's language.
   - telegram: bot with deep-link linking (/start <one-time token>) so the
     guardian opts in; store only chat_id; message includes verdict, tactic
     names, elder's name only if include_elder_name is true, suggested action,
     and 1930 / cybercrime.gov.in for fraud scenarios. No message content.
   - web push: VAPID. Service worker push handler with one-tap actions where
     the platform supports them (Call, View details, Mark reviewed). Document
     that iOS requires the PWA to be installed, and that native Android push
     (FCM) arrives in Layer 5.
6. Delivery transparency: the ELDER can see, in plain language, that an alert
   was sent, to whom, over which channel, and whether it was delivered. No
   hidden alerts.
7. Never auto-contact police or submit complaints. "Report cyber fraud" only
   opens the official info with a confirmation step.

